#align(center, text(size: 7pt, tracking: 0.12em)[ABOUT THIS BOOKLET])
#v(0.08in)

Augustine (354-430) was a teacher of rhetoric from North Africa who became
bishop of Hippo. Around 397, in his early forties, he wrote the
_Confessions_: thirteen books addressed to God, telling the story of his
life up to his conversion and his mother's death, and then turning to
memory, time and creation.

Book One begins with the most famous sentence he wrote: "thou hast made us
for thyself and restless is our heart until it comes to rest in thee." Then
he looks back at what he cannot remember, his infancy, and at what he can,
learning to speak, being beaten at school, loving Virgil more than God. In
the greed of a baby and the tricks of a schoolboy he already sees the
restless heart at work, and grace at work on it.

This booklet is a companion to No. 04, Book Eight, where the restlessness
comes to its crisis in a garden in Milan.

#pagebreak()
#align(center, text(size: 7pt, tracking: 0.12em)[HOW TO READ THIS BOOKLET])
#v(0.08in)

*Read it as a prayer.* The _Confessions_ is addressed to God from the
first line to the last. "Thou" is God; Augustine lets us overhear.

*The numbers.* Each book is divided into chapters (I, II, III...) and
numbered paragraphs (*1*, *2*, *3*...). These are the same in every
edition, so "VIII.12.29" means Book VIII, chapter 12, paragraph 29, whatever
translation the person next to you is holding.

*References* in small grey type, such as #text(size: 6.8pt, fill: luma(110))[(Ps. 139:8)], are the
Scripture Augustine is quoting or echoing. He thinks in the words of the
Bible, above all the Psalms.

*Small raised numbers* point to the Notes at the end.

*The translation* is Albert C. Outler's (1955). He keeps the older "thee"
and "thou" for God, which suits a book that is one long prayer.
